{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":310,"name":"Federico Pianzola","username":"fpianz","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discourse.igelsociety.org/fpianz/{size}/188_2.png","created_at":"2022-07-11T07:27:52.479Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://discourse.igelsociety.org/images/emoji/twitter/speech_balloon.png?v=12\" title=\":speech_balloon:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":speech_balloon:\"\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eSpeaker\u003c/strong\u003e: Richard Jean So\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://discourse.igelsociety.org/images/emoji/twitter/classical_building.png?v=12\" title=\":classical_building:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":classical_building:\"\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eAffiliation\u003c/strong\u003e: McGill University, Canada\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTitle\u003c/strong\u003e: \u003cem\u003eRacial Critique, Data Science, and Literary Studies\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAbstract\u003c/strong\u003e This talk explores the affordances of new methods in data science, such as machine learning, for the analysis of literature and culture. It will argue that a critical and reflexive use of such methods can facilitate new discoveries for literary studies, and that the two paired together can represent an important new form of cultural analysis, particularly for the study of race and literature. The talk is anchored by a case study that explores the post-war US novel, especially Black and Asian American fiction in a comparative racial context.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://discourse.igelsociety.org/images/emoji/twitter/movie_camera.png?v=12\" title=\":movie_camera:\" class=\"emoji only-emoji\" alt=\":movie_camera:\"\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e \u003ca href=\"https://youtu.be/BkRGkAjfaiM\" class=\"inline-onebox\"\u003eRichard Jean So, Racial Critique, Data Science, and Literary Studies - YouTube\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"updated_at":"2022-08-03T18:27:47.296Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":7,"reads":15,"readers_count":14,"score":38.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":246,"topic_slug":"racial-critique-data-science-and-literary-studies","display_username":"Federico Pianzola","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"version":3,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://youtu.be/BkRGkAjfaiM","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"Richard Jean So, Racial Critique, Data Science, and Literary Studies - YouTube","clicks":1},{"url":"https://discourse.igelsociety.org/t/igel2022-conference-schedule/178","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"IGEL2022 conference schedule","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":true,"admin":true,"staff":true,"user_id":1,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"event":null,"calendar_details":[],"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"can_vote":false},{"id":448,"name":"Federico Pianzola","username":"fpianz","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discourse.igelsociety.org/fpianz/{size}/188_2.png","created_at":"2022-07-15T15:15:15.854Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eGreat talk!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou looked at how 2 literary institutions (publishers-bestsellers and critics-prizes) reflect racial biases, I was wondering if looking at a third institution like libraries would show a different pattern?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere are many concurring reasons motivating publishers’ choices but I suspect that librarians (at least in local libraries) are paying more attention to readers’ requests, so this different curatorial attitude may also be reflected in the ratio of “minority” books.\u003c